<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n34417" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pellācĭa</span></b> , ae,
			f.
		pellax, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>an allurement</i>, <i>enticement</i>,
				<i>blandishment.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> In gen. (<b>poet.</b> and very rare; in <i><span class="en">Verg. G. 4,
				443</span></i>, fallacia is the correct read.; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">v. Wagner ad loc.): placidi pellacia ponti,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Lucr.
					5, 1004</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In
			partic., <i>seduction</i> (post-class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Jovialis monumentum pellaciae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Arn. 5, 171</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">4, 147</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
